    meditation:
      "Tomorrow morning, at nine, the 19-year-old sitting two cells down from the holding-tank shower at the federal courthouse will be brought up to Courtroom Five wearing orange. The marshals will not remove the shackles until he is at counsel table. You will not have seen him in three weeks, because the BOP transferred him out of the local jail in May and you could not get over to the contract facility before the calendar broke. The PSR was finalized on Friday. The government's sentencing memorandum was filed Friday night at 11:47 PM. Yours went in at 11:53. The Court has read both.\n\nThis is the night before, and there is nothing left to file. The brief is written. The letters from the grandmother, the youth pastor, the eighth-grade teacher who taught him to read at twelve when no one else had bothered, are in the record. The objections to the offense-level computation are noted, preserved, briefed. You have practiced the allocution with him over the phone, twice, and he has cried both times, and you have told him both times that crying is acceptable and that the judge has seen it before. There is, between the work you have done and the work that will be done tomorrow, only the wait.\n\nThe wait is the part of the practice no one prepared you for in school. The classroom prepared you to draft the motion, to argue the objection, to cross-examine the agent at the suppression hearing. The classroom did not prepare you for the Tuesday evening, alone in your kitchen, after the dishes were done, when there was nothing more to do for the boy who would be sentenced in fourteen hours. You opened the file on the laptop three times. You closed it three times. There was nothing in it that needed to be added. You opened the federal sentencing guidelines manual, the print copy, the one with the coffee ring on chapter five. You did not need it. You knew the range. You had known the range since February.\n\nWhat you are doing in the wait, though you may not name it this way, is something older than the case. You are keeping vigil. The Church has a word for this. The night before a death or a great event, the body is watched, the candle is kept, the soul of someone is held in attention. The lawyer keeps a kind of vigil too, in the night before a sentencing. The boy is not yours by blood, and his family is not your family, and the years he will lose are not your years. But for these hours you carry him, because no one else in the building is carrying him at this hour, and because tomorrow at nine the only person in that courtroom whose job is to stand beside him is you.\n\nThe psalmist's image is exact. \"From the morning watch even until night, let Israel hope in the Lord.\" The morning watch is the last watch of the night, the watch that does not yet see the sun but knows the sun is coming. The advocate at this hour is in that watch. You cannot see the sentence. You know the sentence is coming. The range is forty-six to fifty-seven months. The government will ask for fifty-seven. You will ask for time-served, and if not time-served, then thirty, and if not thirty, then the bottom of the range, with a recommendation that the BOP designate the facility closest to his mother. You have asked for these things in the brief. Tomorrow you will ask for them in voice. The judge will give what the judge will give.\n\nAquinas writes, in the question on clemency, that the clement man is not slow to use the rod; he is slow to use the full weight of it. The line is meant for the judge. The lawyer, tonight, is praying that the judge will be the clement man. There is nothing else the lawyer can do at this hour, which is why prayer is the form the waiting takes whether the lawyer calls it prayer or not. The lawyer who sits at the kitchen table at eleven and reads back through the sentencing memo a fourth time is praying, in his way. The lawyer who walks the dog around the block twice because he cannot sit still is praying, in his way. The lawyer who lies in bed and runs the allocution in his head, the boy's words and his own, is praying, in his way.\n\nWhat you should not do, on this night, is rehearse the worst case until you have memorized it. The temptation is constant, because the worst case is what you are professionally trained to anticipate. You have spent your whole practice anticipating worst cases. Tonight the discipline is different. Tonight the discipline is to have done the work, and then to release the outcome, because the outcome is not yours. The work is yours. The outcome belongs to the court, and behind the court, in a way you cannot prove but cannot quite shake, to God.\n\nThere is one practical instruction the older defenders give the younger ones the night before a sentencing, and it is this: sleep. Sleep if you can. Eat in the morning. Wear the suit you have worn before, the one that does not require adjustment in the courtroom. Get to the courthouse early, but not so early that you stand outside the holding cell for an hour absorbing the boy's fear. He needs you steady. He does not need you wrung-out. The vigil tonight is for you. The morning is for him.\n\nAnd then, when the alarm goes off at five-thirty and the kitchen is cold and the coffee has not yet brewed, you will do the thing that is most useful and most overlooked. You will say a prayer. Not for the outcome. The outcome is set in motion. You will say a prayer for the boy, by name, and for his mother, by name if you know it, and for the judge, by name, and for the prosecutor, by name, and for yourself by no name except servant. The prayer will not change the guidelines table. It will change the lawyer who walks into that courtroom at quarter to nine carrying the file.",
