    meditation:
      "The probation officer's report is the document in the federal criminal practice that most resembles the Book of Life. It runs forty-three pages this time. Half a page on the offense conduct, drawn from the indictment and stipulated facts. Three pages on the criminal history. Two on the guideline calculation. The other thirty-seven, which the judge will actually read on the airplane from D.C. back to chambers tonight, are the life.\n\nThe life is what the offense was committed inside of. The PSR calls it Part C: Offender Characteristics. The sections have numbered headings. Personal and Family Data. Physical Condition. Mental and Emotional Health. Substance Abuse. Educational, Vocational, and Special Skills. Employment Record. Financial Condition. The headings are bureaucratic. The contents are not.\n\nMother, age 17 at birth of defendant. Father, unknown to defendant; defendant has never met him. Defendant raised by maternal grandmother from age 4 to age 11 following mother's incarceration on possession-with-intent. Grandmother died of complications from diabetes when defendant was 11 years and four months old. Defendant returned to mother upon her release. Mother re-incarcerated when defendant was 14. Defendant placed with maternal aunt; left aunt's residence at age 15, having reportedly been struck with a closed fist on multiple occasions. Defendant homeless, intermittently, from age 15 to age 17. Defendant reports first use of opioids at age 15, in the context of a wisdom-tooth extraction performed at a free dental clinic; was prescribed thirty tablets of hydrocodone and was not seen by the clinic again. Educational record: completed eighth grade; attended ninth grade for two months. Tested at fifth-grade reading level at age 16, per records of the youth detention facility. No known learning disability diagnosis on record, though the school district had referred defendant for evaluation three times before defendant left school.\n\nYou read it the first time on the morning the probation officer sent the draft. You were at your desk and the coffee was cold and you got to page eleven before you had to stand up and walk to the window. You read it the second time the next morning, with a pencil, marking the dates that were wrong and the names that were misspelled. You read it a third time three days later, slower, because by then it was not a document to be corrected but a life to be received.\n\nAquinas, on theft, makes the point that the judge who weighs the act without weighing the life weighs unjustly even when his arithmetic is correct. The federal sentencing guidelines are arithmetic. Base offense level. Specific offense characteristics, plus two, plus four, plus a role enhancement that you objected to and lost. Criminal history category, calculated from the prior convictions. Total offense level twenty-three, category three, range fifty-seven to seventy-one months. The arithmetic is real. It will be on the bench tomorrow morning. The arithmetic is also not yet justice, because justice cannot be done to an arithmetic. Justice can only be done to a person, and the person is in Part C.\n\nThis is why the PSR matters more than any other document in the file, more than the indictment, more than the plea agreement, more than the brief you spent three weeks on. The PSR is the place where the life is admissible. Every other document treats the defendant as the legal subject of certain charges. The PSR treats him as a human being who has lived in time, has been formed by particular people, has suffered particular wounds, has made particular choices that were and were not free. The judge will sentence the legal subject. The judge will, if he is the kind of judge the Republic is lucky to have, sentence him in light of the human being.\n\nThe psalm imagines a book in which God writes the days of a life before any of the days have been lived. \"In thy book all shall be written: days shall be formed, and no one in them.\" The psalmist is not making a deterministic claim. He is making a claim about being seen. The life has a witness who has noticed it from before its beginning. The boy whose PSR is on your desk has had, by every visible measure, very few human witnesses. The grandmother who watched him from four to eleven was one. There is a teacher in eighth grade whose letter is in the record. There is the youth pastor at the storefront church on Cottage Grove whose letter is also in the record, three paragraphs, handwritten, ending with \"I have known him since he was 12 and he was always a good boy when he was not afraid.\" That is most of the witnesses. The PSR is the place where these witnesses get assembled into a document the federal system can read.\n\nYour job, reading the PSR a fourth and fifth time, is to know it the way the judge will not have time to know it. The judge will read it once, carefully, on the plane. You will read it as if it were a deposition you are preparing to defend. You will know the dates. You will know the names. You will know the gap between page nineteen and page twenty, where the probation officer compressed eight months of homelessness into one sentence, and you will be ready, if the moment opens, to expand the sentence. You will know which paragraphs the government will quote, and which the government will not, and you will be prepared to direct the court's attention to the paragraphs the government has skipped.\n\nThis is technical work. It is also a kind of reverence. You are reading the life of someone whose life has, until now, mostly not been read. The act of reading it carefully, with a pencil, four times, is an act the law calls preparation. It is also an act older than the law. It is the act of saying, in the only language the courtroom understands, that this is not arithmetic. This is a person, and the days are written in a book older than the guidelines table, and the sentence will be passed on the person and not on the offense level.",
