    weekArc: "Sentencing",
    weekArcLatin: "Sententia",
    title: "The Allocution",
    scripture: {
      reference: "Luke 18:13 (DR)",
      text: "And the publican, standing afar off, would not so much as lift up his eyes towards heaven; but struck his breast, saying: O God, be merciful to me a sinner.",
    },
    excerpt: {
      author: "John Henry Newman",
      citation: "Sermon, 'The Religious Use of Excited Feelings' (Parochial and Plain Sermons, vol. I)",
      text: "It is one of the most painful, yet salutary, of the lessons we learn from life, that words sincerely meant are not always the same thing as words sincerely fulfilled. A man may speak from his heart, and yet not from the depth of his heart; he may say what at the moment he wholly believes, and yet not what governs his subsequent life. The remedy is not to despise such words, for they may be the seed of a better self; nor to trust them wholly, for they may pass; but to receive them gravely, in the hope that what was uttered in a single instant may, by grace, become the labour of years.",
    },
    meditation:
      "Federal Rule of Criminal Procedure 32(i)(4)(A)(ii) reads, in pertinent part, that the court must, before imposing sentence, address the defendant personally in order to permit the defendant to speak or present any information to mitigate the sentence. The rule is bureaucratic. The thing the rule names is not. It is called allocution, from the Latin alloqui, to speak to. It is the moment in the sentencing hearing when the defendant himself, for the first and usually the last time in the case, opens his mouth in the courtroom and says something in his own voice.\n\nHe has rehearsed it with you. You have rehearsed it with him three times. You have told him three things. First, look at the judge. Not at you, not at the floor, not at his mother in the third row, though he will glance there once and that is acceptable. Look at the judge. Second, do not say anything that contradicts the plea. Do not minimize. Do not blame. Do not relitigate. Third, do not memorize a speech. Memorized speeches sound memorized. Have three things you want to say. Know what they are. Say them.\n\nThe first thing is that he is sorry. Not in the abstract, but to the people he hurt. He has to name them, if he can. The mother in the second row. Her son's name, if he can bring himself to say it. He could not say it the first time you rehearsed. He could the second time. The third time he could say it without his voice breaking until the end. The third time was last night, on the phone, with the chaplain sitting next to him at the contract facility. The chaplain is the reason he could say it. He needed someone besides his lawyer to hear it.\n\nThe second thing is that he understands he is being punished, and he is not asking the court not to punish him. He is asking the court for a chance, after, to do something with the rest of his life. He has not had many chances. He does not say it that way, because that would sound like blame, and you have told him not to blame. He says it this way: \"I am twenty years old. I want to be a different man at thirty than I have been at twenty. I am asking the court for the chance to try.\"\n\nThe third thing is what he wants to do, specifically, when he gets out. He has thought about this. He wants to learn HVAC, because his uncle worked HVAC for thirty years and his uncle is the only adult man in his life who held a job that long. He has written to the BOP about whether the facility at Pollock has an HVAC program. He has received an answer that says yes, but only for inmates with five or more years remaining on their sentence. He is asking the court, in his allocution, to give him the kind of sentence that lets him into the program. He does not ask for less time. He asks for the kind of time that does something.\n\nThese are the three things. He will say them in five and a half minutes. You have told him not to try to be eloquent. Eloquence in allocution is suspect, because eloquence sounds prepared, and what the rule wants is not preparation but contrition. The model the rule is reaching for, though the rule does not know it, is the publican in Luke's gospel, standing afar off, not lifting his eyes toward heaven, striking his breast, saying, O God, be merciful to me a sinner. The publican's sentence is six words long. It is the whole of the allocution he gave, and the gospel says he went home justified rather than the other man.\n\nNewman, in the sermon on excited feelings, gives the warning the lawyer must hold even as he prepares the allocution. Words sincerely meant are not always the same thing as words sincerely fulfilled. The boy at counsel table believes, this morning, that he is sorry. He probably is. Whether the sorrow he feels at twenty will be the sorrow he lives at twenty-five is not a thing the courtroom can know. Newman says we are neither to despise such words, because they may be the seed of a better self, nor to trust them wholly, because they may pass. We are to receive them gravely, hoping that what was uttered in a single instant may, by grace, become the labor of years.\n\nThis is the right posture for the judge, and it is also the right posture for the defense lawyer. You are not promising the court, in your client's allocution, that he will become a different man. You are not promising it because you do not know. You are placing before the court the words he has been able to say, and you are asking the court to receive them as the seed Newman names. The seed is not the harvest. The court should not sentence to the harvest. The court should sentence in a way that lets the seed have a chance.\n\nThe prosecutor will, in his own argument, attack the allocution. He will say that the defendant said similar things at a hearing two years ago and was rearrested fourteen months later. He will treat the unfulfilled prior statement as proof that the current statement is also empty. This is the move you have to be ready to answer. Your answer, when you stand, will be that growth is not linear, that a young man who failed at eighteen is not therefore the same young man at twenty, and that the question is not whether the prior allocution was fulfilled but whether the current allocution is honest, and what the conditions of the sentence will be that give it room to become something more than honest.\n\nWhen your client speaks, you will be at counsel table, slightly behind him, because courtroom protocol favors a half-step behind. You will not coach him with your body. You will not nod. You will be still. Your stillness is part of his speaking. The judge will be looking at him. The mother in the second row will be looking at him. The defendant will, if you have prepared him well, be looking at the judge. The sound in the courtroom will be the sound of one young man trying, with the small vocabulary the world has given him, to say what has happened to him and what he hopes might happen next. It will not be eloquent. It will be the seed Newman named. It will be enough.",
