    excerpt: {
      workId: "aquinas-summa",
      author: "Thomas Aquinas",
      citation: "Summa Theologiae I-II, q.95, a.2 (whether every human law is derived from the natural law)",
      text: "Every human law has just so much of the nature of law, as it is derived from the law of nature. But if in any point it deflects from the law of nature, it is no longer a law but a perversion of law. Yet a law may be just in itself and unjust in its application, as when the general form of the law, framed for the common good, in a particular case works against the very good for which it was framed. Then the just man does not despise the law, but he laments its instance, and he prays that the lawgiver may amend what the case has revealed.",
    },
    meditation:
      "There is a moment in some cases when the lawyer reads the indictment, runs the calculation, and realizes that the law is asking him to ask the court for something he cannot in good conscience ask for. The mandatory minimum is ten years. The defendant is nineteen. The offense was non-violent. The co-defendant who actually drove the operation cooperated, signed a 5K1.1 letter, and received four years. Your client refused to cooperate, not out of code-of-silence bravado, but because there was no one above him in the chain to cooperate against. He could not give the prosecutor anything because he did not know anything. So the mandatory minimum, which exists in part to incentivize cooperation, applies to him in its full force, and the cooperator gets four years for organizing, and your client gets ten for being at the bottom of the chain.\n\nThis is not a hypothetical. You have seen it. You have it on your desk right now. The federal sentencing guidelines table you printed at 6 AM is mostly irrelevant in this case, because the statutory minimum supersedes the calculated range, and the calculated range was sixty-three to seventy-eight months anyway, which is below the minimum. Your sentencing memorandum is not really a sentencing memorandum. It is a long, careful, footnoted plea for the court to do something the court is not legally permitted to do. You have argued, in section IV, the safety-valve provision, but the defendant has one prior felony from when he was eighteen, the prior was non-violent, and the statutory text excludes him by one point. You have argued, in section V, that the court should consider a downward variance for what cannot be done lawfully through the guidelines. The judge cannot vary below the statutory minimum. The judge has read your brief. The judge agreed, in the chambers conference, that the sentence is unjust. The judge will impose it anyway, because the judge has no choice. The judge does not have the discretion the system pretends to give. The discretion is held, in this case, by the prosecutor, who made the charging decision in March, and who has not moved off it.\n\nAquinas, in question 95 of the prima secundae, addresses exactly this case. He says that a law may be just in itself and unjust in its application. He says that the just man, in such a case, does not despise the law. He laments its instance. And he prays that the lawgiver may amend what the case has revealed. This is the threefold posture Aquinas commends, and it is the posture the lawyer must hold in his own soul this week, because the other postures available, contempt for the system, cynicism about the practice, abandonment of the case, are all worse.\n\nWhat does it look like, in practice, not to despise the law? It looks like this. You file the brief that argues, with full professional rigor, for the safety valve. You lose. You preserve the issue for appeal. You file the supplemental authority when the Third Circuit issues a decision in another case that may, on a stretch, support a different reading of the statutory text. You lose again. You file the motion for a sentence reduction under the First Step Act when the law changes in 2025. You wait. You file the petition for executive clemency when the administration changes and a new pardon attorney is appointed. You wait. You do not stop being the lawyer of record for this client even after the case is over, because the case is not actually over for him until his sentence is, and a lot of the relief that may eventually be available will require someone to file it on his behalf, and that someone, by the simple fact that you are the lawyer of record, is you.\n\nWhat does it look like to lament the instance? It looks like the honest conversation with the client in the conference room, the morning the plea agreement is signed, in which you tell him that the system in this case is going to do something you believe is wrong, that you are sorry, that you have done everything within the rules to prevent it and have not been able to, and that the wrongness of the result is not on him and not on you and not on the judge but is on the structure of the law itself, which Congress wrote and which Congress will, eventually, you hope, rewrite. You do not pretend the result is just. You also do not pretend it is your fault, or his fault, in ways that are not true. You name what is wrong as wrong, in the room, to the only person to whom your naming it matters.\n\nWhat does it look like to pray that the lawgiver may amend what the case has revealed? It looks like the letter to the senator that you write on a Saturday morning in November, with the case as an example, not naming the client but describing the facts in enough detail that the legislative staffer reading it will recognize what kind of case it is. It looks like the amicus brief you join, two years later, in a Supreme Court case that raises a related question. It looks like the testimony you give, when the bar association asks for volunteers, to the state legislative committee considering similar mandatory minimums for the analogous state offense. It looks like the conversation, at dinner, with the brother-in-law who is a state legislator, in which you do not let the conversation drift to neutral topics and do, instead, describe in plain language what the law has done in the case you cannot name. It looks, in other words, like the lawyer staying in civic life as a witness, not because the witness will fix this case, but because the witness may, over years, fix the next one.\n\nThe deepest temptation in this kind of case is not corruption. It is despair. Despair tells you that the system is irretrievable, that nothing you do matters, that the brief is theater, that the client would be better served by a lawyer who told him the truth and stopped pretending. Despair has the great advantage of feeling, sometimes, like clarity. It is not clarity. It is the soul's exhaustion misnamed. Aquinas's posture, which is not despair, is what carries the lawyer through. Do not despise. Lament. Pray. File. Lose. Preserve. File again.\n\nThis is what Micheas calls walking solicitous with God. Solicitous is the old translation. The Hebrew is closer to careful, or attentive. The lawyer who walks solicitous with God in the mandatory-minimum case is the lawyer who keeps walking, attentive to each motion the case still permits, even when the case appears foreclosed. The walking is the practice. The destination, in this life, is not arrived at. The case ends in a sentence the lawyer believes is unjust, and the lawyer continues, and a future case in the same district, ten years on, may be sentenced under a different rule because someone, somewhere, kept walking. That someone may not be you. The walking nevertheless is.",
